{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779484548,"nonce":"vD8Nl6dv2vu5RDBospHLbeDGnkc=","title":"Long-term nested subgraph inner-iteration semantics","message":"Related: f93db6a, f451fc4, 8d94424, 93f79f2\n\n## Summary\nDefine how a subgraph can intentionally execute an inner rollout or multistep iteration inside one parent graph step, without making nested recurrence ambiguous.\n\n## Motivation\nThe near-term rule is that subgraphs embedded in a parent graph are single-step components: one parent step calls the subgraph once, and any internal recurrent edges advance by the same one simulation step as the parent. That is sufficient for GRU hidden recurrence inside a Network subgraph and for parent loops such as network/mechanics/feedback.\n\nFuture models may need an inner iterative process: solver loops, fixed-count refinement, inner control/planning iterations, sub-rollouts, or multirate dynamics. Those are valid use cases, but they must be represented explicitly rather than hidden behind an ordinary subgraph node.\n\n## Design Questions\n- How does Studio indicate that a subgraph runs `n_inner_steps` or until convergence inside one parent step?\n- How are inner time axes distinguished from the parent rollout time axis?\n- Which state slots and recurrent carries reset at the parent-step boundary versus persist across parent steps?\n- How do manifests and artifacts record nested time axes?\n- Can nested execution be lowered by flattening, or does it require an explicit nested-scan component/operator?\n- How should validation prevent accidental nested scans when the user intended single-step composition?\n\n## Non-goals\n- Do not block recurrent-edge UX or ordinary single-step subgraph recurrence on this issue.\n- Do not implement multirate/fixed-point/acausal solving here.\n\n## Done Criteria\n- A design decision records the allowed inner-iteration forms and their graph/schema representation.\n- Studio has a visual/inspector convention for inner iteration that cannot be mistaken for ordinary recurrent edges.\n- Backend lowering documents how nested time axes and state carry/reset semantics compose.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}